MEDIAPLAYER(1)                   User Commands                  MEDIAPLAYER(1)

NAME
       mediaplayer - play music, videos and internet streams

SYNOPSIS
       mediaplayer  [--fullscreen]  [--no-video]  [--shuffle]  [--repeat=mode]
       [--video-sink=name] [file|folder|playlist|URL...]
       mediaplayer --version

DESCRIPTION
       mediaplayer is the desktop's music and video player, the program behind
       Media Player in the Programs menu and the one a double-clicked song  or
       film  opens in.  It plays whatever the installed GStreamer plug-ins can
       decode: WAV, AIFF and AU sound, AAC and M4A,  MP4,  Matroska  and  WebM
       video  with  H.264,  H.265, VP8 and VP9 pictures, and MP3, Vorbis, Opus
       and FLAC where the FFmpeg decoders  are  enabled.   Streams  play  from
       http,  https and every other kind of address a GStreamer source element
       is installed for, including HLS and DASH.

       The window shows the picture (or, for music, the album  cover)  with  a
       control  bar  below  it,  and  the play queue at the right.  Sound goes
       through autoaudiosink, which picks the system's sound service,  so  the
       player mixes with everything else that is making a sound.

       Each  file,  folder,  playlist  or address given on the command line is
       added to the queue, and the first  one  starts  playing.   Folders  are
       searched  recursively  for  media files, which are queued in name order
       ("Track 2" before "Track 10"), each folder's files before its sub-fold-
       ers.  Hidden  files  and  folders  are  skipped.   M3U,  M3U8  and  PLS
       playlists are read and their entries queued; relative entries are taken
       relative  to the playlist, and Windows-style backslash paths are under-
       stood.

OPTIONS
       -f, --fullscreen
              Start in full screen.

       -a, --no-video
              Play the sound only.  Video streams  are  not  decoded  at  all,
              which saves the processor time a film's pictures would cost; the
              cover-art page is shown instead.

       -s, --shuffle
              Turn  shuffle  on.   Like the button, this is remembered for the
              next start.

       -r, --repeat=mode
              Set the repeat mode: none (or off), all (start the queue over at
              its end) or one (play the current entry again and  again).   Re-
              membered like the button.

       --video-sink=name
              Choose how pictures reach the screen, for this run only:

              auto    The default: the first of xv, gtk and x11 that works.

              xv      The  X  video extension.  The X server (and the graphics
                      hardware behind it) converts  and  scales  the  picture,
                      which  makes  it the cheapest by far.  Needs an Xv adap-
                      tor; xvinfo(1) lists them.

              gtk     Pictures drawn by GTK.   Works  on  every  display,  but
                      scales in software.

              x11     Plain  X  images.  Cannot scale, so the picture is shown
                      at its own size.

              gl      GTK's OpenGL area.  Never chosen automatically.

              If the chosen output cannot be opened, the  automatic  order  is
              tried.

       --version
              Print the program's and GStreamer's versions and exit.

       The standard GTK and GStreamer options are accepted as well (--display,
       --gst-debug-level, and so on); see mediaplayer --help-all.

THE WINDOW
       Seek bar
              Drag  it,  click  it,  or scroll on it.  While it is dragged the
              picture follows  in  key-frame  steps;  the  final  position  is
              reached  exactly  when  it  is let go.  Clicking the time at its
              right switches between the length of the entry and the time  re-
              maining.

       Transport
              Shuffle,  previous, play/pause, next, repeat and stop.  Previous
              restarts the current entry when more than three  seconds  of  it
              have played, and goes to the entry before otherwise.  The repeat
              button cycles through off, all and "this one".

       Volume The slider follows the way loudness is heard (a cubic scale), so
              its  middle sounds like half.  It controls this program's stream
              only; the system volume is audioctl(1).  Turning the  volume  up
              also unmutes.

       Play queue
              Double-click an entry, or select it and press Enter, to play it.
              Entries  are  reordered by dragging them, or with the arrow but-
              tons below the list; Delete removes the  selected  ones.   Files
              and  folders  dropped  on the list are inserted where they fall;
              dropped on the picture they are added and played at  once.   Ti-
              tles,  artists and lengths of queued local files are read in the
              background before they play.  The right  mouse  button  opens  a
              menu with the same actions.

       Menu   Open  files, folders, locations and playlists; save the queue as
              a playlist; choose the audio track and the subtitles of  a  film
              with  several;  set the playback speed (the pitch is kept); show
              the media information window, the  keyboard  shortcuts  and  the
              version.

       Full screen
              Double-click  the  picture,  press  F or F11, or use the button.
              The control bar slides away while the pointer  rests  and  comes
              back when it moves; Esc leaves full screen.

       The  middle  mouse button on the picture plays or pauses, the right one
       opens the menu, and the scroll wheel changes the volume.

KEYBOARD
       Space                 Play or pause.

       N, P                  Next  or  previous  entry  (also  Ctrl+Right  and
                             Ctrl+Left, and the media keys).

       Left, Right           Back  or  forward  5  seconds;  with  Shift,  one
                             minute.

       Home                  Back to the beginning.

       0 ... 9               Jump to 0 %, 10 % ... 90 % of the entry.

       Up, Down              Volume up or down by 5 %; also + and -.

       M                     Mute or unmute.

       S                     Shuffle on or off.

       R                     Repeat: off, all, this one.

       [, ]                  Slower or faster (0.5x to 2x); Backspace  returns
                             to normal speed.

       F, F11                Full screen on or off.

       Esc                   Leave full screen.

       T                     Show the length or the time remaining.

       I, Ctrl+I             Media  information:  tags,  codecs, picture size,
                             sample rate, and which video  and  sound  outputs
                             are in use.

       F9                    Show or hide the play queue.

       Delete                Remove  the  selected  queue  entries  (while the
                             queue has the keyboard).

       Ctrl+O                Open files; Ctrl+Shift+O opens a folder.

       Ctrl+L                Open a location (an internet address).

       Ctrl+S                Save the queue as a playlist.

       F1                    The list of shortcuts.

       Ctrl+Q                Quit.

       While the queue has the keyboard (after it is clicked), the arrow  keys
       move  through  it;  clicking the picture gives them back to seeking and
       the volume.

ERRORS
       An entry that cannot be played is marked in the queue and explained  in
       a  dialog,  with  the  technical  details  folded  away  beneath.  When
       GStreamer reports that it needs a plug-in that is  not  installed,  the
       dialog names it -- "MPEG-1 Layer 3 (MP3) decoder", for example.  A film
       whose  pictures  play  but whose sound (or subtitles) cannot be decoded
       keeps playing, with a note above the picture.

       When the queue moves on by itself and the next entry fails, that  entry
       is  skipped with a note instead of stopping everything behind a dialog,
       unless every entry in turn has failed.

FILES
       ~/.config/mediaplayer/settings
              What is remembered between runs: volume and  mute,  shuffle  and
              repeat,  the window and queue sizes, whether the queue is shown,
              the folder the file chooser last opened, and the keys below.   A
              key  file with one [Player] group, written with mode 0600.  Keys
              that are missing, mistyped or out of range fall  back  to  their
              defaults one by one.

              video-sink          The default for --video-sink.

              gapless             true  (the  default)  to run consecutive en-
                                  tries together without a pause.

              discover-metadata   false to stop titles and lengths  of  queued
                                  files from being read ahead.

              subtitles           false to start with subtitles hidden.

       /usr/share/applications/mediaplayer.desktop
              The desktop entry: the applications menu, and the file manager's
              "Open With" list.

ENVIRONMENT
       MEDIAPLAYER_VIDEO_SINK
              Used  instead  of the remembered video-sink when no --video-sink
              is given.

       GST_DEBUG
              GStreamer's own diagnostics, as for every GStreamer program.

EXIT STATUS
       0      The window was closed.

       1      The display could not be opened, or GStreamer  (or  its  playbin
              element) is missing.

       2      A bad option.

SEE ALSO
       audioplay(1), audioctl(1), eventsound(1), xvinfo(1)

